body:
I see ~two~ one issue~s~:

- **A table schema version mismatch during a transaction does not cause an eventual refresh when that transaction is over.**  This is not the case with (global) catalog version mismatch because, for catalog version, it is checked every query/txn boundary (see `YBCheckSharedCatalogCacheVersion` in the postgres.c loop step 7).  For table schema version mismatch, if it is detected in a transaction, the transaction fails (there is no transaparent retry) and this knowledge of the table schema version mismatch isn't stored anywhere.  So the next transaction that accesses the table will also get table schema version mismatch.  Only when accessing it outside a transaction will the cache refresh happen.

  `YBPrepareCacheRefreshIfNeeded`:

  ```c
      if (YBNeedRetryAfterCacheRefresh(edata))
      {
          ...
          if (consider_retry &&
                  !IsTransactionBlock() &&
                  !YBCGetDisableTransparentCacheRefreshRetry())
          {
  	    ...
              /* Refresh cache now so that the retry uses latest version. */
  ```

  A fix could be to store this knowledge so that we refresh the cache on next query/txn boundary or invalidate the table's cache entry right there because the transaction is aborted anyway.  Also keep in mind the other conditions like `YBNeedRetryAfterCacheRefresh`, `consider_retry`, `YBCGetDisableTransparentCacheRefreshRetry`.  These retry-related conditions should not block cache refreshes for future queries that aren't retries.
- ~**The table (docdb) schema cache on tserver pg_client_service is not properly invalidated on global cache refresh.**  The table schema is cached in postgres pg_session as `table_cache_` and tserver pg_client_service as `table_cache_` which wraps `cache_`.  A breaking catalog version bump causes a global cache refresh using `YBRefreshCache`, which calls `YBCPgInvalidateCache`, `PgApiImpl::InvalidateCache`, `PgSession::InvalidateAllTablesCache`, `table_cache_.clear();` for the invalidation.  Any future uses of the table will try to open/load it, calling `PgSession::LoadTable` which does~

  ```cpp
    auto cached_table_it = table_cache_.find(table_id);
    bool exists = cached_table_it != table_cache_.end();
    if (exists && cached_table_it->second) {
      return cached_table_it->second;
    }
  
    VLOG(4) << "Table cache MISS: " << table_id;
    auto table = VERIFY_RESULT(pg_client_.OpenTable(table_id, exists, invalidate_table_cache_time_));
  ```

  ~Notice the `exists` variable, which, if you follow down, determines if the tserver pg_client_service will refresh (reopen) its table cache entry.  In case of `PgSession::InvalidateAllTablesCache`, the `table_cache_` entry for the table will not exist, so a refresh will not be requested on tserver's cache.  In case of a table schema version mismatch (that did not happen in a transaction), we call `YBCPgInvalidateTableCacheByTableId`, `PgApiImpl::InvalidateTableCache`, `PgSession::InvalidateTableCache` which requests to refresh (reopen) the tserver's table cache.~

  ```cpp
    if (invalidate_on_pg_client) {                                                                          
      // Keep special record about this table_id, so when we would open this table again,                   
      // reopen flag will be sent to pg client service.                                                     
      // This flag means that pg client service should remove table from his cache and fetch                
      // new data from master.                                                                              
      // It is optional optimization, but some tests fails w/o it, since they expect that
      // local table information is updated after alter table operation.                                                                                                                 
      table_cache_[table_id] = nullptr;
  ```

  ~In short, global catalog refresh does not completely refresh the table schema cache but table schema version mismatch does.~

  ~I believe the tserver's pg_client_service table cache should stay up-to-date.  That was probably the assumption so that we could avoid redundant reopens of the tserver's table cache whenever each postgres backend gets a table schema version mismatch.~  `InvalidateAllTablesCache` sets `invalidate_table_cache_time_` which will make sure tserver's cache is refreshed.
